{
  "body_human_readable": "Archimedes of Syracuse (c. 287 \u2013 c. 212 BC) was a preeminent ancient Greek mathematician, physicist, engineer, inventor, and astronomer. He is widely regarded as one of the leading scientists in classical antiquity and one of the greatest mathematicians of all time.\n\nBorn in the city of Syracuse on the island of Sicily, his father was an astronomer named Phidias. It is believed that Archimedes studied in Alexandria, Egypt, with the successors of Euclid when he was young.\n\n**Scientific and Mathematical Contributions:**\n\nArchimedes is renowned for a wide range of discoveries and innovations. His major contributions include:\n\n*   **Archimedes' Principle:** This fundamental law of hydrostatics states that a body immersed in a fluid is buoyed up by a force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. Legend has it that he discovered this principle while in his bath and ran through the streets naked, shouting \"Eureka!\" (I have found it!).\n*   **Mathematics:** He applied concepts of infinitesimals and the method of exhaustion, which anticipated modern calculus, to prove various geometric theorems. He calculated a remarkably accurate approximation of pi (\u03c0) and developed a system for expressing very large numbers. He also discovered that the volume of a sphere is two-thirds the volume of a cylinder that encloses it, a finding he considered his most significant.\n*   **Physics:** Archimedes established the law of the lever and pulleys, which allows heavy objects to be moved with minimal force. He also did significant work on the concept of the center of gravity.\n\n**Inventions:**\n\nArchimedes was a prolific inventor, credited with creating numerous practical devices:\n\n*   **The Archimedes Screw:** This device, a screw enclosed in a pipe, efficiently raises water and is still used today for irrigation and in sewage treatment plants. He may have invented it after a visit to Egypt.\n*   **War Machines:** During the Roman siege of Syracuse, Archimedes designed innovative defensive machines. These included cranes to drop rocks, claws to lift enemy ships out of the water, and catapults to fire missiles. It is also said he devised a system of mirrors to focus sunlight and set Roman ships on fire.\n*   **Odometer:** He is credited with conceiving the first odometer, a device to measure distance traveled.\n\n**Death and Legacy:**\n\nArchimedes died during the Siege of Syracuse in 212 or 211 BCE. Despite orders from the Roman general, Marcus Claudius Marcellus, that he should not be harmed, a Roman soldier killed him. One account suggests that Archimedes was engrossed in a mathematical problem drawn in the sand and told the soldier, \"Don't disturb my circles.\" As he had requested, his tomb was marked with a sphere and a cylinder to commemorate his favorite mathematical discovery.",
